From: Kervin Pursoty <kpursoty@proton.me>
Subject: clocksource: timer-econet-en751221: fix block mapping and IRQ ack

Fix two bugs in the EN751221 timer driver affecting EN751627:

1. Block mapping at boot (NULL membase panic)

timer_init() used DIV_ROUND_UP(num_possible_cpus(), 2) to count register
blocks. At early boot with VPE-based SMP, MIPS reports num_possible_cpus()=1
(VPEs not yet online), giving num_blocks=1. Only membase[0] is mapped.

On EN7528/EN751627 with 4 VPEs, cevt_init_separate() iterates over IRQs
0-3 and calls cevt_dev_init(i) for each. cevt_dev_init(2) accesses
membase[2>>1] = membase[1] which is NULL, panicking:

  CPU 0 Unable to handle kernel paging request at virtual address 00000008
  epc : iowrite32+0x4/0x10
  ra  : cevt_dev_init+0x40/0x64

Fix: replace the runtime calculation with ECONET_NUM_BLOCKS, which is
DIV_ROUND_UP(NR_CPUS, 2) at compile time. For NR_CPUS=4 this is always 2,
correctly mapping both blocks regardless of VPE visibility at boot.

2. IRQ ack triggers interrupt storm

The handler acked by copying count back to compare:

  iowrite32(ioread32(reg_count(cpu)), reg_compare(cpu));

Since count >= compare fires the interrupt, and the counter advances past
the written value before the MMIO reaches the peripheral, the condition
re-asserts on the same bus cycle. This causes a tight storm on CPU0.

The EcoNet GPL kernel (arch/mips/econet/time2.c, cputmr_timer_ack()) acks
by writing the *next* deadline, which puts count < compare and clears the
pending condition. For one-shot mode write U32_MAX (unreachable before
set_next_event() programs the real deadline), consistent with cevt_dev_init()
initialisation.

Signed-off-by: Kervin Pursoty <kpursoty@proton.me>
--- a/drivers/clocksource/timer-econet-en751221.c
+++ b/drivers/clocksource/timer-econet-en751221.c
@@ -85,7 +85,9 @@
 	if (!cevt_is_pending(cpu))
 		return IRQ_NONE;

-	iowrite32(ioread32(reg_count(cpu)), reg_compare(cpu));
+	/* Ack: write a future value (U32_MAX) so count < compare, clearing the
+	 * pending condition.  set_next_event() will program the real deadline. */
+	iowrite32(U32_MAX, reg_compare(cpu));
 	dev->event_handler(dev);
 	return IRQ_HANDLED;
 }
@@ -248,7 +248,6 @@

 static int __init timer_init(struct device_node *np)
 {
-	int num_blocks = DIV_ROUND_UP(num_possible_cpus(), 2);
 	struct clk *clk;
 	int ret, i;

@@ -260,7 +259,7 @@

 	econet_timer.freq_hz = clk_get_rate(clk);

-	for (i = 0; i < num_blocks; i++) {
+	for (i = 0; i < ECONET_NUM_BLOCKS; i++) {
 		econet_timer.membase[i] = of_iomap(np, i);
 		if (!econet_timer.membase[i]) {
 			pr_err("%pOFn: failed to map register [%d]\n", np, i);
@@ -297,7 +296,7 @@
 	return 0;

 err_unmap:
-	for (i = 0; i < num_blocks; i++) {
+	for (i = 0; i < ECONET_NUM_BLOCKS; i++) {
 		if (econet_timer.membase[i])
 			iounmap(econet_timer.membase[i]);
 	}
